WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  parents students and teachers are  eagerly preparing classrooms in mexico's  guerrero state for a return to in-person  classes  for the last 18 months thomas alva  edison elementary school in the  outskirts of acapulco has remained  closed the effort underway is to have  the facility up and running by the  august 30th back-to-school deadline  i hope we can return to classes so that  our children can study and have an  interest in their education because it's  not the same to study at home  though everyone is lending a hand many  here are worried the effort may not be  enough parents say the facility lacks  many basic necessities like sanitation  supplies and at times even running water  the school needs many things as you can  see even the wooden windows are rotting  away  coronavirus contagion is still quite  high in this part of mexico and so are  concerns over going back to school  we have to get the school in good  condition with good ventilation and  enough space as you can see the  classrooms are quite small  social distancing is complicated  the reopening of schools has been a  divisive issue in mexico sparking  protests against the government where  many claim authorities have mismanaged  the response to the pandemic  this demonstration in downtown mexico  city was organized by students and their  parents many of whom say they want a  return to in-person classes but are  concerned that the mexican government  cannot guarantee the safety of students  government statistics suggests more than  5 million children have dropped out of  school during the pandemic children's  rights organizations also point to other  alarming trends like a 24 increase in  cases of domestic abuse  international organizations like unicef  and the who say fears over contagion are  warranted but also warn of the negative  impacts the last year and a half has had  on children and adolescents  we believe the risk of reopening schools  is lower than the risks children and  adolescents are already running by  keeping them out of school education is  not only about knowledge it's about  learning social skills it's a question  of development it's a matter of reaching  one's potential in life and we have to  prioritize this  though most schools in mexico began  welcoming students on monday morning  many others have decided not to reopen  thomas alva edison elementary in  guerrero state is among those choosing  to keep their doors closed until  coronavirus contagion rates in the state  are adequately reduced  manuel rapalo al jazeera mexico city

